Just take, like, a smaller subsection of, like, you know, hey, here's eight fiefs.

00:20:04.720 --> 00:20:15.920
Like, here's, like, your assignment is just to, like, you know, befriend another one and try to take down this one or, you know, have the most rice at the end of the year or something.

00:20:15.920 --> 00:20:24.060
Like, I would have preferred smaller missions to have, like, goals to work towards the ultimate goal of, like, a larger conquest game.

00:20:24.200 --> 00:20:30.600
I think that that would help smooth out the learning curve at the very least, and I would probably have played that mode.

00:20:31.980 --> 00:20:48.440
Like, even if I was playing this on my own as, like, this is my tutorial and maybe just for a shorter play session, not play session, but, like, total play time to actually see a victory or a defeat.

00:20:49.760 --> 00:20:57.780
But I think, like, the issue, my issue would still remain, and it doesn't really matter how long or complex the game is.

00:20:57.920 --> 00:21:01.020
It's just really hard to interact with.

00:21:02.140 --> 00:21:14.500
And in terms of the graphics, I mean, I don't know if cleaner is the word, but it does seem brighter, and there are some animations now on some of these actions that weren't in the first game.

00:21:14.500 --> 00:21:19.760
But it's really just, like, a nice new coat of paint on the original.

00:21:19.760 --> 00:21:21.860
It feels more update than sequel.

00:21:22.040 --> 00:21:25.600
It doesn't look like they didn't change the menu layouts or anything.

00:21:25.600 --> 00:21:32.400
They just kind of made, like, they just kind of redrew some things to be like, hey, we know how to do trees better now, so we'll draw the trees nicer.

00:21:32.400 --> 00:21:51.000
Well, it's also, um, they kind of zoomed out instead of these massive, like, they almost look like cards that sort of served as each hex or, I guess, square that you can travel to in weird directions.

00:21:51.700 --> 00:21:54.000
Um, like, it kind of looks like a card game.

00:21:54.000 --> 00:21:55.080
Oh, you're talking about for, like, the combat screens?

00:21:55.080 --> 00:21:55.880
Yeah, in combat.

00:21:56.080 --> 00:21:57.020
Yes. Yeah, yeah.

00:21:57.120 --> 00:22:04.560
Now they've zoomed out, and it actually just looks like tiles in a strategy game, which I think is visually closer to what I'm used to.

00:22:05.020 --> 00:22:16.120
Um, but they're also, like, shrink down a lot of the art and, um, change some of the, how some of the information is portrayed.

00:22:17.340 --> 00:22:19.420
And I think it's an overall improvement.

00:22:19.420 --> 00:22:28.300
There's also a day-night cycle for some reason now, um, which, uh, I think really just changes how much of the map you could see.

00:22:28.560 --> 00:22:31.720
And for those parts that you can see, it makes it darker.

00:22:32.440 --> 00:22:41.920
Um, but other than, yeah, I think that that is a good enough improvement for, like, that kind of iterative update that we were talking about.

00:22:41.920 --> 00:22:53.980
Whether that's sequel territory or not, I'm not sure, but it does, like, include, like, that first mode, uh, is basically just Nobunaga's ambition in this engine.

00:22:54.240 --> 00:23:01.100
So I guess the, it's, like, just more Nobunaga, like, because now you have that other later campaign.

00:23:02.200 --> 00:23:10.480
That, um, that new combat screen you're talking about, though, one thing that is funny about it is, uh, you know, if you have, um, horses, right?

00:23:10.480 --> 00:23:15.280
It'll have, like, a little drawing of a horse, and it'll have the number of troops, uh, in that unit.

00:23:15.640 --> 00:23:18.760
So you'll have a picture of a horse and, like, the number 20.

00:23:19.220 --> 00:23:23.820
And then you'll, uh, think of, like, any other kind of tactic-style game.

00:23:23.840 --> 00:23:28.440
You'll have your unit, and you can move it a certain amount of spaces depending on its speed.

00:23:28.960 --> 00:23:35.840
And when you do that, it opens up this other menu that, given the way, uh, like, just the way that the game is drawn and everything,

00:23:36.000 --> 00:23:39.200
it covers half the battlefield with this menu.

00:23:39.200 --> 00:23:46.640
Yeah. And I just think that, like, at least it's smart enough to know, like, where your unit is and which side it should cover,

00:23:46.640 --> 00:23:52.080
so that it doesn't block, like, information of you if you're directly attacking a different unit.

00:23:52.200 --> 00:23:57.120
But if the units start on the left side of the screen and you have to get over to the right,

00:23:57.580 --> 00:24:02.120
it is gonna block the enemies that you're trying to, like, see to attack.

00:24:02.340 --> 00:24:04.840
Like, you have to almost, like, have a mental picture of them for a while,

00:24:04.840 --> 00:24:07.540
because that menu is going to cover those other units.

00:24:07.740 --> 00:24:10.360
And it's just like, guys, there's gotta be a better way.

00:24:10.500 --> 00:24:14.580
Yeah, just, you know, I appreciate that you're showing more of the map now.

00:24:14.820 --> 00:24:21.460
That is still an improvement if you just, like, keep some real estate dedicated to the UI.

00:24:21.740 --> 00:24:24.800
Like, you don't have to have these pop-up things covering the map.

00:24:24.800 --> 00:24:29.080
Just have the bottom of the screen be the menu all the time.

00:24:31.020 --> 00:24:40.180
There is a interesting bug in the game where if you view your samurai and see how many fief they have,

00:24:40.560 --> 00:24:46.220
the fief number displays one lower than what it actually is.

00:24:46.220 --> 00:24:55.660
So, if you look at Kozuke Province 2, it shows up as Province 1.

00:24:56.100 --> 00:25:01.700
But if you go to the other view where you view daimyos instead of samurais,

00:25:01.940 --> 00:25:03.200
the number is correct.

00:25:03.300 --> 00:25:06.140
So, it's just, like, it's off by one.

00:25:06.140 --> 00:25:08.880
And that's, like, something that is very trivial.

00:25:08.880 --> 00:25:10.820
And, again, like, someone like me wouldn't notice.

00:25:11.040 --> 00:25:13.860
But, like, would affect gameplay, right?

00:25:13.860 --> 00:25:16.580
Like, you're trying to, like, have a map of, like, who's who.

00:25:17.300 --> 00:25:19.760
And the samurai view is off by one.

00:25:20.000 --> 00:25:20.840
That's not great.

00:25:21.320 --> 00:25:22.720
Wait, so is this, like, a...

00:25:22.720 --> 00:25:26.640
This is actually described as a bug by the community?

00:25:26.980 --> 00:25:28.420
I think it's, yeah, by the community.

00:25:28.420 --> 00:25:31.020
Because if you look at, like, the daimyos one is correct.

00:25:31.200 --> 00:25:33.860
Like, it would tell you which province they're correctly in.

00:25:34.060 --> 00:25:36.820
Whereas the samurai view will have them off by one.

00:25:37.420 --> 00:25:39.300
That's... Like, to the point where it's basically impossible.

00:25:39.300 --> 00:25:43.100
Like, if it's province one, it'll say that they're on province zero.

00:25:43.100 --> 00:25:44.160
Which doesn't exist.

00:25:44.520 --> 00:25:45.940
Yeah, that's silly.

00:25:46.100 --> 00:25:48.040
I thought that you meant that it would...

00:25:48.040 --> 00:25:54.320
It was telling you that they owned one less, like, province than they did.

00:25:55.500 --> 00:25:58.320
Not, like, the actual label of the province.

00:25:58.620 --> 00:26:01.640
That's weird. I mean, I guess it's just, you know...

00:26:03.100 --> 00:26:04.220
They tried...

00:26:04.220 --> 00:26:07.520
Like, one part of it is in normal people speak.

00:26:07.520 --> 00:26:11.560
And the other side of it is in, like, programmer speak.

00:26:12.440 --> 00:26:13.260
Right, yes.

00:26:13.380 --> 00:26:14.300
Yeah, that's weird.

00:26:14.760 --> 00:26:17.760
And then just, like, you know, here's, like, an observation.

00:26:19.240 --> 00:26:22.000
The controls of the game, which we cannot get into.

00:26:22.100 --> 00:26:24.920
We can't say everything that the A button does and all that.

00:26:25.180 --> 00:26:26.700
Like, this isn't that kind of game.

00:26:26.980 --> 00:26:29.120
But start and select do nothing.

00:26:29.820 --> 00:26:31.520
Yeah, I was confused by that, too. And that's just kind of surprising.

00:26:31.860 --> 00:26:33.460
Yeah. Right? Like, think about it.

00:26:33.540 --> 00:26:37.400
Like, you could have mapped, like, you know, a list of hotkeys or something.

00:26:37.680 --> 00:26:40.620
You know, like, actions that you would want to do more often.

00:26:40.900 --> 00:26:45.740
Just press the start button and it'll pull up, like, some default things that you might want to do.

00:26:45.840 --> 00:26:49.440
And then you could just select across those rather than navigating a series of menus.

00:26:49.720 --> 00:26:52.120
I just think it's funny that you don't use them at all.

00:26:52.260 --> 00:26:57.020
Like, a game with so many menus and start and select, they're like, nah, we don't need anything for those.

00:26:57.780 --> 00:27:04.440
It did lead to some confusion of, like, there are some menus that I think are points of no return.

00:27:04.440 --> 00:27:08.580
Like, if you get to them, you cannot undo what you're about to do.

00:27:10.080 --> 00:27:14.060
Like, maybe if you just deselect everything, you can just back out.

00:27:14.380 --> 00:27:20.060
But the confirm, we're getting a little into the weeds here.

00:27:20.220 --> 00:27:21.700
No, I like getting into the weeds.

00:27:21.700 --> 00:27:30.400
If you're about to go into battle and you're choosing, like, the generals that you want to bring with you, you can't exit that.

00:27:32.400 --> 00:27:37.780
Okay, I will clarify and say, like, if you have selected any generals, you can't back out.

00:27:37.780 --> 00:27:42.880
Because the back out button is now the move forward and fight button.

00:27:45.100 --> 00:27:49.200
Because your confirm button is now the select generals thing.

00:27:49.200 --> 00:27:50.400
There's no, like, submit.

00:27:51.280 --> 00:27:52.820
I found it very strange.

00:27:53.160 --> 00:28:00.660
Maybe if I were to just unselect everybody, it would let me, like, turn back from the ledge.

00:28:00.960 --> 00:28:03.060
But it felt weird otherwise.

00:28:03.760 --> 00:28:05.800
You know why I like when you get in the weeds?

00:28:06.680 --> 00:28:08.600
Because I say nonsense?

00:28:09.300 --> 00:28:11.840
You make the game sound as exciting as it is.

00:28:12.760 --> 00:28:13.880
Yeah, yeah.

00:28:14.800 --> 00:28:19.260
When you talk like that and trying to explain to somebody, it would be like anybody.

00:28:19.260 --> 00:28:20.460
And it's not a you problem.

00:28:20.600 --> 00:28:24.420
It's like anybody trying to explain what is interesting about this game.

00:28:24.480 --> 00:28:32.460
Because it could be, right? Like, you could have the story in your head of, like, man, there was, like, I had 200 less troops than the other samurai.

00:28:32.460 --> 00:28:34.100
And I went into battle with this guy.

00:28:34.220 --> 00:28:36.860
And I just grinded him down.

00:28:37.040 --> 00:28:38.620
Like, you know, like, it was amazing.

00:28:38.620 --> 00:28:40.360
And it didn't matter in the long run.

00:28:40.360 --> 00:28:43.080
And Oda still had his way in Congo, Japan.

00:28:43.340 --> 00:28:46.080
But for one day, I had that other thief.

00:28:46.080 --> 00:28:48.740
And it's like, no one else is going to find that story any interesting.

00:28:48.980 --> 00:28:52.500
It's like trying to describe an epic Pokemon battle you had with the Elite Four.

00:28:52.580 --> 00:28:53.820
Oh, I know that you've done that.

00:28:54.300 --> 00:28:59.740
Yeah. But speaking of grinding, that is what these battles kind of feel like.

00:28:59.840 --> 00:29:05.240
Because, and again, I cannot remember if it was like this in the first one.

00:29:05.240 --> 00:29:17.480
But I don't remember in the battle screens going, like, once you hit the fort, going to another map that zooms in.

00:29:17.540 --> 00:29:20.100
And you have to travel another map all the way.

00:29:20.100 --> 00:29:24.560
Like, it takes so, like, you get so few moves with each of your units.

00:29:25.680 --> 00:29:31.160
And there is such a lag between selecting all of your units.

00:29:31.880 --> 00:29:35.480
That it, getting just across the map takes forever.

00:29:35.480 --> 00:29:49.760
And then just getting to the fort on the big map and then seeing a kind of labyrinthine, like, zoomed in fort map replace it is kind of disheartening.

00:29:49.780 --> 00:29:51.760
Because I thought I, like, had the guy cornered.

00:29:52.020 --> 00:29:55.780
And now I have a whole other map to navigate.

00:29:56.320 --> 00:29:57.700
Did you feel this way?

00:29:58.480 --> 00:30:03.700
It's disheartening because these battles are, like, forget about how many moves it might take to get to that.

00:30:03.700 --> 00:30:06.560
And it's a grind because it's also so long.

00:30:06.740 --> 00:30:08.300
And it's not just, like, I get it.

00:30:08.380 --> 00:30:12.940
They're trying to make it also so this way you're not just, like, thinking, I should just battle everything.

00:30:13.160 --> 00:30:17.740
Like, this is a game where you just fight other daimyos and just continue to take land.

00:30:17.860 --> 00:30:22.040
Like, that's the way. Like, no, this game is a resource management thing first.

00:30:22.040 --> 00:30:25.720
And then, like, only when you're confident you're going to win should you go into a fight.

00:30:25.800 --> 00:30:30.960
You shouldn't risk fighting because you're going to, you know, you probably run out of rice before you finish the battle.

00:30:30.960 --> 00:30:33.820
But these battles, we're talking, like, I don't know.

00:30:33.940 --> 00:30:35.400
We're talking, like, 30 minutes.

00:30:35.660 --> 00:30:36.660
Yeah. Or more.

00:30:36.820 --> 00:30:37.940
Right? Yeah. Or more.

00:30:38.120 --> 00:30:39.340
Yeah. It's crazy.

00:30:39.680 --> 00:30:41.200
It's way too long of a battle.

00:30:41.360 --> 00:30:44.720
I'm currently playing Fire Emblem Fortune's Weave.

00:30:44.840 --> 00:30:46.460
And it's a very long game.

00:30:46.540 --> 00:30:56.780
But the one thing I like is that the battles themselves, these tactic-style battles, are very quick unless they are the end of a chapter battle in which, of course, you know, like, they're epic.

00:30:56.780 --> 00:30:58.560
And you have to fight, like, 60 different units.

00:30:58.700 --> 00:31:04.220
But for the most part, you're just, like, running into some, like, you know, like, here's six people.

00:31:04.360 --> 00:31:05.860
Here's your overpowered team.

00:31:06.080 --> 00:31:11.320
Like, just, you know, make sure you take them without taking too much damage because you have to keep fighting to explore the rest of the land.

00:31:11.480 --> 00:31:15.160
Like, they're nice little digestible battles that I can do on a train ride.

00:31:15.260 --> 00:31:16.400
But even when...

00:31:16.400 --> 00:31:17.940
In this game, I can't do on a train ride.

00:31:17.940 --> 00:31:24.500
Yeah. But even when those are longer, there's a bit more going on than just attack.

00:31:25.080 --> 00:31:27.680
Well, yes. Because every unit is specialized.

00:31:27.900 --> 00:31:29.040
Like, that's what this is missing, too.

00:31:29.200 --> 00:31:37.520
It's, like, you go in there and you have, like, 12 different squares that are all the same horse unit just with different numbers attached to them.

00:31:37.680 --> 00:31:38.920
You can have rifle guys, too.

00:31:39.580 --> 00:31:40.620
Sure. You know what I mean, though.

00:31:40.620 --> 00:31:49.060
It's, like, they're all, like, multiple of these same units and you're just moving each one of them across the screen for a while to get from point A to point B.

00:31:49.220 --> 00:31:54.260
It needed to be, like, hey, here it is all on one map, just like Fire Emblem is, right?

00:31:54.360 --> 00:31:55.440
Like, it's all on one map.

00:31:55.780 --> 00:31:56.780
Like, you got this.

00:31:56.920 --> 00:32:00.340
You just... If you clear the screen, you won the battle.

00:32:00.340 --> 00:32:07.760
But, of course, with Nobunaga's ambition and all these grander strategy games, nothing is ever so easy.

00:32:07.760 --> 00:32:12.020
And also, probably for its target audience, doesn't want to be.

00:32:12.320 --> 00:32:20.940
No. This is not the north versus south where you have different controls for your cannons and your people.

00:32:21.320 --> 00:32:26.120
I kind of wish it was that because that was just ridiculous enough to be funny each time.

00:32:27.300 --> 00:32:35.140
Whereas, yeah, this is a very plotting strategy X battle game.

00:32:35.140 --> 00:32:41.220
And after you win one of these battles, every captured samurai has one of three options.

00:32:41.220 --> 00:32:44.920
You can behead them, you can recruit them, or you can release them.

00:32:46.100 --> 00:32:55.880
Recruit a man and, you know, the advice is probably strip him of his troops until his loyalty is brought back up because, you know, he's not loyal to you yet.

00:32:55.880 --> 00:33:00.280
So, he could do mutiny and take his troops with him and you don't want that.

00:33:00.280 --> 00:33:01.220
He could do a mutiny?

00:33:01.940 --> 00:33:04.280
Yeah. A rebel, you know?

00:33:04.400 --> 00:33:09.340
Yeah. So, you got to, like, kind of, like, earn his loyalty first and then you can give him back his troops.

00:33:10.020 --> 00:33:12.900
You release him, he's just going to wind up in someone else's army.

00:33:12.900 --> 00:33:13.840
So, that's kind of stupid.

00:33:13.840 --> 00:33:16.240
So, you might as well behead him rather than release him.

00:33:16.240 --> 00:33:28.460
But the real trick, as I learned in one of these game FAQ guides, is if you've got a samurai with either terrible stats or bad loyalty that you don't want to raise back up, don't dismiss him.

00:33:28.460 --> 00:33:39.620
If you hand him a single soldier, send him to war and have him charge so he dies in the war rather than doing a normal attack.

00:33:39.620 --> 00:33:41.220
They added a charge attack to this game.

00:33:41.380 --> 00:33:44.900
If you have him charge, he dies instead of being captured.

00:33:45.800 --> 00:33:47.240
Ah. Great, great game.

00:33:47.500 --> 00:33:52.220
Wow. Yeah, this is some next level, like, Geneva Convention stuff.

00:33:53.000 --> 00:33:55.140
Well, there's another thing I picked up on a guide.

00:33:55.420 --> 00:34:02.920
Selling a large amount of food drops the market price by, like, a noticeable amount in the game.

00:34:03.040 --> 00:34:05.380
It's a percentage, so I'm not quite sure what the percentage is.

00:34:05.380 --> 00:34:09.000
But if you sell a large amount of food, it drops it.

00:34:09.100 --> 00:34:11.320
If you buy a large amount of food, it raises it.

00:34:11.880 --> 00:34:13.880
So, sell your whole stock.

00:34:14.160 --> 00:34:15.460
The price drops.

00:34:15.840 --> 00:34:22.620
Then you buy it all back for the same money plus the extra that you sold it for because now it's worth less.

00:34:22.920 --> 00:34:25.680
And then you just continue to repeat until you have infinite rights.

00:34:25.700 --> 00:34:26.620
Infinite money glitch.

00:34:26.860 --> 00:34:34.360
Okay. Well, the first story reminded me a lot of, like, you know, a strategy that some people would use in Crusader Kings.

00:34:34.360 --> 00:34:46.020
Like, if you had a really bad heir, like, if your first son had bad stats, had, like, some weird congenital trait, and nobody in the realm liked him.

00:34:46.380 --> 00:34:49.260
But your second son was, like, super cool.

00:34:50.120 --> 00:34:51.200
He's a genius.

00:34:52.680 --> 00:34:54.980
Better. You want to play as him next?

00:34:54.980 --> 00:34:58.680
You could just have your heir lead an army very similarly.

00:34:58.920 --> 00:35:03.820
But just put him on a boat and then just have the boat just, like, wait out in the ocean forever.

00:35:03.820 --> 00:35:05.280
And he'll eventually die.

00:35:05.840 --> 00:35:07.180
So, yeah.

00:35:07.400 --> 00:35:17.020
It's funny how 35, 30-something years later, it's the same kind of weird metagaming with war crimes.

00:35:17.540 --> 00:35:19.280
Just send him out on a boat.

00:35:19.460 --> 00:35:20.640
The Natalie Wood strategy.

00:35:46.820 --> 00:35:48.220
Thank you.

00:36:20.420 --> 00:36:23.220
Thank you.

00:36:46.220 --> 00:36:49.020
Thank you.

00:36:49.020 --> 00:36:58.080
It's like basically just a turn skip because fortifying your castle, it's the only thing in the build option that doesn't raise happiness.

00:36:58.560 --> 00:37:02.280
So you could do anything else and your villagers will like you more.

00:37:02.360 --> 00:37:04.120
They don't want you to fortify your castle.

00:37:04.280 --> 00:37:07.220
For reasons I can understand, but I guess that it's like you live in the castle.

00:37:07.360 --> 00:37:08.400
So, of course, you're happy.

00:37:08.500 --> 00:37:09.660
But now the villagers are angry.

00:37:10.300 --> 00:37:15.840
There's got to be like a good reason like if you are besieged, right?

00:37:16.480 --> 00:37:18.980
Right. That's exactly when you should do it.

00:37:19.240 --> 00:37:24.500
But I guess, you know, the alternative is also just use those funds to buy more men.

00:37:25.020 --> 00:37:26.900
Ah, yes. Recruit.

00:37:27.680 --> 00:37:30.560
Not buy. The game is solved, Sean, as you can tell.

00:37:30.780 --> 00:37:36.360
You know, this is AI hasn't yet solved chess, but they have definitely solved Nobunaga's ambition.

00:37:36.680 --> 00:37:38.040
Is that a fact? Have you been watching?

00:37:38.240 --> 00:37:39.240
No, they have not.

00:37:39.380 --> 00:37:48.660
But have you ever watched like those low-level learning machine things like play video games and see how they like get over time?

00:37:48.780 --> 00:37:57.440
Like I haven't seen it with a strategy game, but I have watched someone do it with like Mario Kart, Super Mario Kart on the Super Nintendo, the first iteration of Mario Kart.

00:37:57.440 --> 00:38:07.720
And that was like interesting to watch it basically go from like it would drive in a straight line and just hit a wall to like, okay, now I know there's a wall there.

00:38:07.860 --> 00:38:11.420
So, I got to turn and then like, you know, speed that up 500 times and eventually.

00:38:12.240 --> 00:38:19.240
